We're becoming more edibly advanced these days. I'm a new recruit to the 
Bioforge team, and their favorite food is sushi. The Pacific Strike team loved 
Mongolian BBQ. On Serpent Isle, we lived for Chicago-style deep-dish pizza 
(Mangia). As for beverages, it was Shiner Bock (sp?) last night...

>Theres a demo of a game called Myst kicking around the net, that has a
>quicktime movie showing how the game was put together, going from the designers
>story boards, to the graphics designers building the visuals, creating the
>sound effects, music, incorporating 'live' action and then finally putting the
>whole product together. Pretty impressive (and it was done with Hypercard???).
>I guess theres not really many one-man outfits around these days producing top
>quality games software (like Jeff Minter back in the good'ol days).

No, the major games are put together by very large teams (20-30 people), many 
of them specialists in diverse areas. You would also be surprised by the 
number of people it takes to create missions or role-playing environments.

Remember also that this is becoming big business, with lots of money at stake 
(which means lots of money to spend, as well). CyberHollywood.

>How would a company like Origin go about putting a games team togther? Would it 
>rely on existing in-house talent? If it wanted to bring people in, would they
>go by established reputation or on the basis of unsolicited demo code/artwork/
>music/scripts? 

Depends on the producer. Of course, you use in-house people first if you have 
them, but in a growth industry that's not always possible. On my first project 
(Serpent Isle), most of us were green!

